Year = {2025},
Volume = {119},
Number = {1},
Pages = {332-348},
Month = {FEB},
Abstract = {How does the representation of women in international organizations
   affect the implementation of gender mainstreaming policies? Many
   international organizations have adopted policies to prevent gender
   discrimination in their operations, but their implementation is often
   lackluster. We argue that these shortcomings appear due to a combination
   of institutional incentives and an underrepresentation of women in their
   staff. We test the argument in the case of the World Bank, drawing on
   highly disaggregated staffing data, an instrumental variable strategy,
   and an elite survey experiment. Our results show that most staff
   incorporate at least shallow gender mainstreaming in their projects.
   Deeper implementation of gender mainstreaming is more likely when women
   staff supervise projects, hold positions of authority, and are more
   represented as coworkers. These results contribute to understanding the
   disconnects between talk and action on mainstreaming policies and inform
   debates on representation in global governance.},
